---
/* Apple's own artwork, from the App Store Marketing Tools bundle
   (Download_on_the_App_Store_Badge_US-UK_RGB_blk_092917). The file in
   src/assets is the only copy of it: Astro renders an imported .svg as a
   component, so the paths live in exactly one place rather than being pasted
   in here as well. The guidelines forbid recreating, modifying, angling or
   animating the badge, so nothing here scales or transitions it.
   https://developer.apple.com/app-store/marketing/guidelines/

   Inlined rather than <img src>: it keeps the badge a same-origin asset with
   no extra request, which is what lets the privacy policy claim the site makes
   no third-party requests at all. Apple requires you use their artwork, not
   that you hotlink it.

   One change from Apple's file, made in the .svg itself, and only one: their
   paths colour themselves with style="fill: #fff", and the CSP nginx serves
   sets style-src 'self' with no 'unsafe-inline' -- which blocks the style
   ATTRIBUTE as well as <style>. Blocked, every fill falls back to the SVG
   default of black and the badge renders as a solid black slab (invisible in
   dev, which serves no CSP). So each one is the presentation attribute
   fill="#fff" instead: same colours, same paths, no inline CSS. If you ever
   re-download this badge from Apple, redo that conversion --
   test/output.test.ts fails if you don't.

   Rendered at its native 40px -- Apple's stated onscreen minimum. The
   wrapper's padding is the required clear space, one quarter of the badge
   height. */
import Badge from "../assets/app-store-badge.svg";
import { APP_STORE_URL } from "../config";
---

<a
  class="inline-block rounded-lg p-2.5"
  href={APP_STORE_URL}
  aria-label="Download Barklog on the App Store"
>
  <Badge />
</a>
